1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,640
Tesla showed its Roadster doing this.

2
00:00:02,650 --> 00:00:04,540
Then, hanging upside down.

3
00:00:04,550 --> 00:00:08,508
The trick is air, but two
completely different systems.

4
00:00:08,550 --> 00:00:10,190
One sticks it to the track.

5
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:12,630
The other throws it around in midair.

6
00:00:12,950 --> 00:00:15,240
So what did that actually prove?

7
00:00:15,550 --> 00:00:17,240
First, the grip.

8
00:00:17,250 --> 00:00:20,340
Tesla says fans pull air
out from under the floor.

9
00:00:20,350 --> 00:00:23,740
Seal the edges, and the air
left in that gap thins out.

10
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:26,361
Not to a vacuum, just lower pressure.

11
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:29,400
So the air outside pushes
harder than the air underneath,

12
00:00:29,410 --> 00:00:32,240
and that difference presses
the car against the track,

13
00:00:32,250 --> 00:00:36,656
always into the surface, even upside down,
where gravity pulls straight down,

14
00:00:36,666 --> 00:00:38,176
away from the ramp.

15
00:00:38,250 --> 00:00:41,020
Tesla says the fans
can pull up to two g's.

16
00:00:41,030 --> 00:00:43,880
On paper, that's about
twice the car's weight.

17
00:00:43,950 --> 00:00:46,240
But it only works while the seal holds.

18
00:00:46,250 --> 00:00:48,840
At the end of the ramp,
the surface runs out.

19
00:00:48,850 --> 00:00:52,540
Air floods back into the gap,
and the grip is gone.

20
00:00:52,550 --> 00:00:55,040
Up here, wheels and fans can't help.

21
00:00:55,050 --> 00:00:57,340
That's where the second system comes in.

22
00:00:57,350 --> 00:01:00,420
Tesla says these thrusters,
built with help from SpaceX,

23
00:01:00,430 --> 00:01:02,140
run on compressed air.

24
00:01:02,150 --> 00:01:05,240
Open a valve and gas
blasts out of a nozzle.

25
00:01:05,250 --> 00:01:08,640
Gas goes out one way,
the car gets pushed the other way.

26
00:01:08,650 --> 00:01:11,847
No fuel, no flame, just cold gas.

27
00:01:11,950 --> 00:01:13,469
Now push off-center.

28
00:01:13,479 --> 00:01:16,940
One side up, the other side
down, and the car rolls.

29
00:01:16,950 --> 00:01:19,740
Fire the opposite pair and the roll stops.

30
00:01:19,750 --> 00:01:21,480
Then aim the gas down.

31
00:01:21,490 --> 00:01:24,380
That pushes the car
upward and slows the fall.

32
00:01:24,450 --> 00:01:28,476
To truly hover, the thrust would
have to match the car's whole weight.

33
00:01:28,750 --> 00:01:30,090
Here's the real one.

34
00:01:30,100 --> 00:01:31,550
It rolls upright,

35
00:01:32,550 --> 00:01:35,420
then the jets fire down
to soften the landing.

36
00:01:35,550 --> 00:01:37,640
The same push works on the ground.

37
00:01:37,650 --> 00:01:41,160
Tesla says thrusters at the back
shove the car forward at launch,

38
00:01:41,170 --> 00:01:43,680
harder than the tires alone could grip.

39
00:01:43,850 --> 00:01:48,440
On the stream, a recorded run was
shown at 1 second on its own timer.

40
00:01:49,100 --> 00:01:51,881
Tesla claimed 0.81 seconds.

41
00:01:51,891 --> 00:01:54,101
Nobody timed that one independently.

42
00:01:54,550 --> 00:01:57,540
Tesla lists three versions
built up like this.

43
00:01:57,550 --> 00:01:59,940
Performance gets the fans for grip.

44
00:01:59,950 --> 00:02:02,640
Thrust adds rear thrusters for launches.

45
00:02:02,650 --> 00:02:06,940
Rocket adds jets all around, and it's
the only one Tesla lists with flight.

46
00:02:06,950 --> 00:02:10,193
Tesla says that one isn't
approved for public road use.

47
00:02:10,450 --> 00:02:12,240
The catch is the air.

48
00:02:12,250 --> 00:02:14,540
Every burst spends what's in the tank.

49
00:02:14,550 --> 00:02:19,240
Tesla says onboard compressors refill it,
but hasn't said how long that takes.

50
00:02:19,250 --> 00:02:21,300
And nobody was steering from inside.

51
00:02:21,310 --> 00:02:24,000
Musk said the car drove
itself for the demo.

52
00:02:24,150 --> 00:02:26,340
Two systems, two jobs.

53
00:02:26,350 --> 00:02:28,140
The fans hold it to a surface.

54
00:02:28,150 --> 00:02:30,480
The jets change its motion in the air.

55
00:02:30,550 --> 00:02:33,300
Tesla showed a spectacular stunt.

56
00:02:33,350 --> 00:02:37,740
How long it can stay up and how repeatable
that is are still the questions.

57
00:02:37,750 --> 00:02:38,964
Clever engineering?

58
00:02:38,974 --> 00:02:40,240
Absolutely.

59
00:02:40,250 --> 00:02:41,252
Your next commute?

60
00:02:41,262 --> 00:02:43,192
That's a much bigger leap.
